      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Peasants were legally bound to the manor and could not freely choose trading partners or terms.",
      "evidence": "Serfdom restricted mobility; peasants required the lord’s permission to leave the manor and were subject to the lord’s courts, limiting any independent commercial activity.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Lords monopolised essential facilities (mills, ovens, markets) and imposed mandatory fees (banalities).",
      "evidence": "Manorial records list compulsory payments for using the lord’s mill or oven, and many lords held market rights that required peasants to pay tolls even at fairs.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Statutory law (e.g., the Statute of Labourers 1351) fixed wages and restricted peasant mobility, reinforcing the power imbalance.",
      "evidence": "The Statute of Labourers capped wages after the Black Death and mandated that laborers accept the terms set by their lord, showing state‑backed coercion.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1, 2, 3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Fair trade requires voluntary, equitable exchange between autonomous parties, which was absent in the feudal system.",
      "evidence": "Modern definitions of fair trade stress free bargaining, equal footing, and transparent pricing—conditions not met because peasants lacked freedom of choice, faced compulsory dues, and were subject to legal restraints.",
